        "Each of these `librosa` features carries specific psychoacoustic or signal-processing meaning. Our model's hypothetical features, while influenced by general concepts like 'energy' or 'valence' during simulation, do not have a one-to-one, scientifically precise correspondence with any particular `librosa` feature. A direct, scientifically accurate mapping would essentially mean that our hypothetical features are specific, known combinations or transformations of these `librosa` features, which isn't the case here.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why a direct mapping is not straightforward:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  **Semantic Gap:** The hypothetical features are abstract, while `librosa` features are concrete measurements of audio signals. There's no clear, objective way to say \"MFCC[0] is `feature_1`\" or \"spectral centroid + RMS energy = `feature_2`\" without prior knowledge or empirical study linking them to the training data generation process.\n",
